### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to produce an immutable list from a stream. This tests knowledge of `Collectors.toUnmodifiableList`, `collectingAndThen`, `Stream.toList`, and the differences between them.

### How do you collect into an unmodifiable list with Java 10+?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class UnmodifiableListDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Employee.getSampleEmployees();

        // Java 10+: toUnmodifiableList()
        List<String> names = employees.stream()
            .map(Employee::getName)
            .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList());

        System.out.println("Names: " + names);

        // This throws UnsupportedOperationException:
        try {
            names.add("Rajesh");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("Cannot modify: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Names: [Rajesh, Priya, Amit, Kavita, Suresh, Deepak, Neha, Vikram, Ananya, Meera]
Cannot modify: UnsupportedOperationException
```

### How do you do it in Java 8 with collectingAndThen?

```java
// Java 8 portable form
List<String> namesJava8 = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.collectingAndThen(
        Collectors.toList(),
        Collections::unmodifiableList));

// Same result: add/remove/set/clear all throw UnsupportedOperationException
```

`collectingAndThen` first collects into a mutable `ArrayList`, then applies the finisher function `Collections::unmodifiableList` which wraps it in a read-only view.

### How does Stream.toList() work (Java 16+)?

```java
// Java 16+: shortest form
List<String> namesJava16 = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getName)
    .toList();  // returns unmodifiable list

// Equivalent to collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList()) in behavior
```

### How are the three approaches different?

| Feature | toUnmodifiableList() | collectingAndThen | Stream.toList() |
|---|---|---|---|
| Java version | 10+ | 8+ | 16+ |
| Null elements | Throws NPE | Allows nulls | Allows nulls |
| Backing reference | None (truly immutable) | Wrapper over ArrayList | None (truly immutable) |
| Conciseness | Medium | Verbose | Most concise |
| Type | Unmodifiable List | Unmodifiable view | Unmodifiable List |

```java
// toUnmodifiableList: rejects null
try {
    List<String> withNull = Stream.of("Rajesh", null, "Priya")
        .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList());  // NPE!
} catch (NullPointerException e) {
    System.out.println("Null rejected by toUnmodifiableList");
}

// Stream.toList: allows null
List<String> withNullOk = Stream.of("Rajesh", null, "Priya")
    .toList();  // [Rajesh, null, Priya] - works
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: assuming `Collectors.toList()` returns an unmodifiable list.

```java
// WRONG assumption: toList() returns a MUTABLE ArrayList
List<String> mutable = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
mutable.add("NewName");  // works! list is mutable

// CORRECT: use toUnmodifiableList() for immutability
List<String> immutable = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList());
immutable.add("NewName");  // UnsupportedOperationException
```

Mistake 2: confusing `Stream.toList()` (Java 16) with `Collectors.toList()` (Java 8).

```java
// Stream.toList() -> unmodifiable
List<String> unmod = employees.stream().map(Employee::getName).toList();

// Collectors.toList() -> mutable
List<String> mod = employees.stream().map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// They look similar but have different mutability guarantees!
```

### When should you use unmodifiable lists?

- Return values from service methods: prevents callers from corrupting shared state.
- Configuration lists: loaded once, read many times, should never change.
- Audit trails: once recorded, entries must not be added or removed.
- Thread safety: an unmodifiable list is safe to share across threads without synchronization.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ComplianceService {
    private final List<Employee> employees;

    public ComplianceService(List<Employee> employees) {
        this.employees = List.copyOf(employees);  // defensive copy
    }

    // Returns unmodifiable list: callers cannot corrupt internal state
    public List<Employee> getHighValueAccountHolders() {
        return employees.stream()
            .filter(e -> e.getSalary().doubleValue() > 1_000_000)
            .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList());
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between unmodifiable and immutable?" An unmodifiable view can still change if the backing collection changes. A truly immutable list (from `toUnmodifiableList` or `List.of`) has no backing reference.
- "How do you create an unmodifiable Set or Map?" Use `toUnmodifiableSet()` or `toUnmodifiableMap()`.
- "What if you need to modify the list later?" Collect with `Collectors.toList()` to get a mutable ArrayList.
